Here is the root cause:
The code-generation template in TableRenderer.java produced static factory/hydrator inner classes (e.g., anonymous Function implementations and byte-array hydrators) that called `of(...)` or `new Xxx(...)` without qualifying it with the enclosing Row/Column class name. Since these calls occur inside static nested classes, the unqualified `of(...)` reference is ambiguous or resolves incorrectly (or fails to compile in certain nesting contexts), rather than explicitly invoking the intended `RowClass.of(...)`/`ColumnClass.of(...)` factory method. Additionally, the template lacked a way to statically expose the raw (unnamespaced) table name and used wildcard-inducing/missing imports (`Hashing`, `ValueType`), causing further generation inconsistencies. Because all table classes are mechanically generated from this single template, the same incorrect pattern was replicated across every generated table file (UserProfileTable, UserPhotosStreamHashAidxTable, UpgTaskMetadataTable, etc.).

